        <div class="blog-prose">
          <h2 id="how-esi-is-calculated">How ESI is calculated</h2>

          <p>Employees' State Insurance is a health-insurance contribution, and unlike PF it is calculated on <strong>gross monthly wages</strong> rather than Basic. It applies only where those wages fall at or below the <strong>₹21,000 per month ceiling</strong>.</p>

          <p>Where it applies, the contribution splits as <strong>0.75% from the employee</strong> and <strong>3.25% from the employer</strong> — a combined 4% of wages. On wages of ₹18,000, that is ₹135 from the employee and ₹585 from the employer.</p>

          <p>ESI amounts are rounded <strong>up</strong> to the next rupee, not to the nearest one. It is a small difference, but it is the reason a hand calculation can disagree with a payslip by a rupee.</p>

          <h3>The eligibility rule that catches people out</h3>

          <p>ESI does not switch off the moment someone crosses ₹21,000. Coverage runs in fixed <strong>contribution periods</strong>, and an employee who is covered at the start of a period generally stays covered until that period ends — even if a mid-period increment pushes their wages above the ceiling.</p>

          <p>So an employee who gets a raise in the middle of a contribution period usually continues to have ESI deducted for the remaining months. Stopping the deduction the same month is a common and consequential error, because it leaves the employee's coverage record inconsistent with what was actually paid.</p>

          <p>The calculator above answers the simpler question — is this wage within the ceiling — and cannot know an individual's coverage history. Verify that history before you stop deducting for anyone.</p>

          <h3>Who ESI covers</h3>

          <p>ESI applicability depends on the establishment as well as the individual. Coverage generally turns on employee headcount and the notified areas the establishment operates in, so a company below the threshold may have no ESI obligation at all regardless of individual wages. Confirm your establishment's coverage before applying the rates to anyone.</p>

          <h2 id="esi-on-the-payslip">ESI on the payslip and after</h2>

          <p>The employee's 0.75% is a payslip deduction. The employer's 3.25% is a company cost that sits in CTC without reducing take-home pay — the same structure as <a href="<%= pf_calculator_path %>">PF</a>, and modelled on both sides by the <a href="<%= ctc_calculator_path %>">CTC to in-hand calculator</a>.</p>

          <p>Contributions withheld become a liability due within 15 days of the close of the wage month. For that deadline alongside PF and TDS, and where ESI sits in the month-end sequence, see the <a href="<%= blog_post_path("payroll-processing-india") %>">complete guide to payroll processing in India</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%= render "resources/calculator_footer",
